===================================================
    
Case 1: the || operator returns the cast value padded to n     

===================================================
id    cast(c_double as char(10))||'|'    cast(c_float as char(10))||'|'    cast(c_numeric as char(10))||'|'    
1     1.5       |     1.5       |     1.5       |     
2     -1.5      |     -1.5      |     -1.5      |     
3     null     null     null     

===================================================
    
Case 2: CONCAT and CONCAT_WS return the cast value padded to n     

===================================================
id    concat( cast(c_double as char(10)), '|')    concat_ws(',',  cast(c_double as char(10)), '|')    
1     1.5       |     1.5       ,|     
2     -1.5      |     -1.5      ,|     
3     null     |     

===================================================
id    concat( cast(c_float as char(10)), '|')    concat_ws(',',  cast(c_float as char(10)), '|')    
1     1.5       |     1.5       ,|     
2     -1.5      |     -1.5      ,|     
3     null     |     

===================================================
id    concat( cast(c_numeric as char(10)), '|')    concat_ws(',',  cast(c_numeric as char(10)), '|')    
1     1.5       |     1.5       ,|     
2     -1.5      |     -1.5      ,|     
3     null     |     

===================================================
    
Case 3: RPAD, REPEAT and REVERSE read the pad spaces of the cast value     

===================================================
id    rpad( cast(c_double as char(10)), 12, '*')    repeat( cast(c_double as char(10)), 2)    reverse( cast(c_double as char(10)))    
1     1.5       **     1.5       1.5                   5.1     
2     -1.5      **     -1.5      -1.5                 5.1-     
3     null     null     null     

===================================================
id    rpad( cast(c_numeric as char(10)), 12, '*')    repeat( cast(c_numeric as char(10)), 2)    reverse( cast(c_numeric as char(10)))    
1     1.5       **     1.5       1.5                   5.1     
2     -1.5      **     -1.5      -1.5                 5.1-     
3     null     null     null     

===================================================
    
Case 4: REPLACE, INSERT and SUBSTR read the pad spaces of the cast value     

===================================================
id    replace( cast(c_double as char(10)), '5', '7')    insert( cast(c_double as char(10)), 2, 1, 'x')    substr( cast(c_double as char(10)), 1, 5)||'|'    
1     1.7            1x5            1.5  |     
2     -1.7           -x.5           -1.5 |     
3     null     null     null     

===================================================
id    replace( cast(c_numeric as char(10)), '5', '7')    insert( cast(c_numeric as char(10)), 2, 1, 'x')    substr( cast(c_numeric as char(10)), 1, 5)||'|'    
1     1.7            1x5            1.5  |     
2     -1.7           -x.5           -1.5 |     
3     null     null     null     

===================================================
    
Case 5: the cast value has n characters and n bytes     

===================================================
id    char_length( cast(c_double as char(10)))    octet_length( cast(c_double as char(10)))    char_length( cast(c_float as char(10)))    octet_length( cast(c_float as char(10)))    char_length( cast(c_numeric as char(10)))    octet_length( cast(c_numeric as char(10)))    
1     10     10     10     10     10     10     
2     10     10     10     10     10     10     
3     null     null     null     null     null     null     

===================================================
    
Case 6: a number as long as n gets no pad spaces     

===================================================
cast(c_double as char(3))||'|'    cast(c_float as char(3))||'|'    cast(c_numeric as char(3))||'|'    
1.5|     1.5|     1.5|     

